  "summary": "Systematic classification of children's drawing development from scribbling to representational forms.",
  "description": "Characterized by progressive stages from uncontrolled scribbles to basic shapes and eventually representational forms, reflecting cognitive development and motor skills. Documented through developmental psychology research, notably by Viktor Lowenfeld and Rhoda Kellogg, building on Kandinsky's early observations.",
  "culturalContext": "Child art developmental stages — Rhoda Kellogg's 6,000 scribble analysis, Viktor Lowenfeld's stages (pre-schematic 4-7, schematic 7-9, dawning realism, pseudo-naturalism) — classify universal drawing evolution: the X-ray transparency of the child's family portrait, the baseline ground line, the big-head figure (tadpole person). Art education built on the research; contemporary painters (Basquiat, Dubuffet) deliberately quoted the stages, and developmental-stage fluency is now standard pedagogy.",
